  "text": "umably, we should start by understanding the geometry involved. The sphere rolls without slipping along a helical path on a cylindrical surface. Each complete turn of the sphere brings it back to its original position, which means that the sphere travels a distance equal to the circumference of its circular path in each turn.\n\nThe vertical distance $h$ between consecutive turns is the height of one full turn of the helix. If the sphere completes 13 turns, it will have traveled a vertical distance of $13h$.\n\nThe horizontal distance traveled by the sphere in one complete turn is the circumference of the circle traced by the center of the sphere. This distance is $2\\pi R$ since the radius of the sphere is $R$.\n\nThe vertical distance between the center of the sphere and the axis of the cylinder is always $d$. This means that the helix has a vertical component of $d$ and a horizontal component of $2\\pi R$ per turn.\n\nTo find the ratio $\\frac{R}{r}$, we need to relate the total vertical distance traveled by the sphere to the distance it has traveled horizontally. Since the sphere returns to its original position after 13 turns, the total vertical distance traveled is $13h$, and the total horizontal distance traveled is $13 \\times 2\\pi R = 26\\pi R$.\n\nThe vertical distance between the sphere's center and the axis of the cylinder is always $d$. This means that the helix has a vertical component of $d$ and a horizontal component of $2\\pi R$ per turn. Since the sphere completes 13 turns, the total vertical distance traveled is $13d$.\n\nTherefore, we have the equation:\n\\[ 13h = 13d \\]\n\\[ h = d \\]\n\nNow, we need to relate the total horizontal distance traveled to the circumference of the circle traced by the center of the sphere. Since the sphere returns to its original position after 13 turns, the total horizontal distance traveled is $26\\pi R$. The circumference of the circle traced by the center of the sphere is $2\\pi r$.\n\nTherefore, we have the equation:\n\\[ 26\\pi R = 2\\pi r \\times 13 \\]\n\\[ 26\\pi R = 26\\pi r \\]\n\\[ R = r \\]\n\nHowever, we need to express the ratio $\\frac{R}{r}$ in the form $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{p}{q}}$, where $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime integers. Since $R = r$, the ratio is:\n\\[ \\frac{R}{r} = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{1}} = \\sqrt[3]{1} = 1 \\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]",
